From 87581e58755f8787d721c3be24503cc8f0bb1bc1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xFFshell <>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 10:18:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] humanize: lean the gaze's yaw onto the corner, not its pitch
 onto the floor

GazeAhead leaned the walking target onto the line the path takes next in both
axes. The line is the angle from the eyes to the centre of the next block, about
thirty degrees down on level ground, while the walking target keeps the player's
own pitch - so the class's premise, that on a straight line the two directions
are the same and the lean is nothing, held for the yaw and not for the pitch. With
every walking movement one block long the player is always inside the two-block
lead distance, so the lean was on for the whole of every flat walk and held the
head on the floor two blocks ahead: measured in the client with the new per-tick
trace, a level start settled at a median of 29 degrees down, 93% of the samples
past fifteen, against HUMANLIKE.md's "near level most of the time".

The lean is now of the yaw alone; the pitch is PitchWander's, and the slope lean's
where the path climbs or falls. In the client the same walk has a median pitch of
0.5 to 2.6 degrees and none of its samples past fifteen. thePitchLeansToo, which
pinned the old behaviour, becomes thePitchIsNeverLeanedOnto, with a test that a
straight flat walk is no lean in either axis; restoring the pitch blend fails both
and nothing else. HumanlikeLookFuzzTest's expected pitch (8.5, half of the way to
the offer) is the walk's own 5 now, and the spec row says why.
